  "transcribed_text": " A recovery decision starts with the affected workload  and the authority to act.  Consider two independent training groups, A and B.  Each has four participants.  Group A has a committed checkpoint after step 42.  It completes steps 43 and 44,  then loses a participant during step 45.  Under this example's supported recovery policy,  A stops as a group.  B continues because it has no dependency on A's recovery.  The controller correlates the job failure  with device and network evidence.  A compatible spare is available,  but availability alone doesn't establish readiness.  The controller must check health, communication,  and the required power and cooling capacity,  then restore the training state.  Restoring the checkpoint returns A to the end of step 42.  Completed steps 43 and 44 must be repeated,  along with the unfinished work from 45.  This is explicit recovery from a known state.  After validating restored state and group membership,  the controller resumes at step 43.  Retry limits and an escalation path bound further attempts.  Now consider an expansion request needing 40 kilowatts  where the approved facility capacity  has only 30 kilowatts of headroom.  The request remains pending,  even if more devices are idle.  Both figures refer to the same illustrative facility boundary.  Define the recovery group, restore valid state,  and verify capacity before admitting work.  Broaden the response only when evidence  identifies shared dependencies or wider failure.",
